<div class="page">
  <header class="masthead">
    <p class="kicker">Java Notes</p>
    <h1>Chapter — Working with Dates and Times</h1>
    <p class="sub">Create dates and times, write them in any style, and measure the gap between them.</p>
  </header>
  <hr class="rule" />
  <p class="hero-statement">A date is easy to <em>read</em> and surprisingly easy to get <em>wrong</em>.</p>
  <p class="hero-sub">Birthdays, deadlines, exam timetables, bell schedules: dates and times are everywhere. Java&rsquo;s <code class="inline">java.time</code> package gives you ready-made classes that know how many days are in each month, which years are leap years, and how to write a date in any style you like.</p>
  <div class="flow-box">
    <span class="step">Create a date or time</span><span class="arrow">↓</span>
    <span class="step">Read it or change it</span><span class="arrow">↓</span>
    <span class="step">Write it the way people expect</span><span class="arrow">↓</span>
    <span class="step">Measure the gap between two moments</span>
  </div>
  <section class="sec" id="d01"><span class="sec-num">01</span><h2>Meet the <code class="inline">java.time</code> package</h2><p class="lede">Java comes with a built-in package called <code class="inline">java.time</code> that holds ready-made classes for working with dates and times. You don&rsquo;t install anything; you just <code class="inline">import</code> the class you need. Six classes do almost all the work. Click each box to see what it&rsquo;s for.</p><div class="hierarchy">
  <svg viewBox="0 0 720 340" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" role="img" aria-label="Map of the six java.time classes">
    <text x="360" y="14" text-anchor="middle" style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.07em;fill:#948E84">STORE A DATE / TIME</text>
    <path class="hline" d="M 120 76 L 120 108 L 360 108 L 360 136"/>
    <path class="hline" d="M 360 76 L 360 136"/>
    <path class="hline" d="M 600 76 L 600 108 L 360 108 L 360 136"/>
    <path class="hline" stroke-dasharray="5 4" d="M 50 76 L 50 268 L 90 268"/>
    <path class="hline" stroke-dasharray="5 4" d="M 670 76 L 670 268 L 630 268"/>
    <text x="360" y="204" text-anchor="middle" style="font-family:'Inter',sans-serif;font-size:11px;font-weight:700;letter-spacing:.07em;fill:#948E84">MEASURE THE GAP</text>
    <g class="hnode" data-node="LocalDate"><rect x="30" y="26" width="180" height="50"></rect><text x="120" y="48" text-anchor="middle">LocalDate</text><text x="120" y="63" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">year · month · day</text></g>
    <g class="hnode" data-node="LocalDateTime"><rect x="270" y="26" width="180" height="50"></rect><text x="360" y="48" text-anchor="middle">LocalDateTime</text><text x="360" y="63" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">date + time together</text></g>
    <g class="hnode" data-node="LocalTime"><rect x="510" y="26" width="180" height="50"></rect><text x="600" y="48" text-anchor="middle">LocalTime</text><text x="600" y="63" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">hour · minute · second</text></g>
    <g class="hnode" data-node="DateTimeFormatter"><rect x="190" y="136" width="340" height="50"></rect><text x="360" y="158" text-anchor="middle">DateTimeFormatter</text><text x="360" y="173" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">format() to text · parse() from text</text></g>
    <g class="hnode" data-node="Period"><rect x="90" y="240" width="220" height="56"></rect><text x="200" y="263" text-anchor="middle">Period</text><text x="200" y="279" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">gap between dates (y · m · d)</text></g>
    <g class="hnode" data-node="Duration"><rect x="410" y="240" width="220" height="56"></rect><text x="520" y="263" text-anchor="middle">Duration</text><text x="520" y="279" text-anchor="middle" class="sub">gap between times (seconds)</text></g>
  </svg>
  <div class="hierarchy-legend">
    <div class="hierarchy-chip" data-node="LocalDate">LocalDate</div><div class="hierarchy-chip" data-node="LocalTime">LocalTime</div><div class="hierarchy-chip" data-node="LocalDateTime">LocalDateTime</div>
    <div class="hierarchy-chip" data-node="DateTimeFormatter">DateTimeFormatter</div><div class="hierarchy-chip" data-node="Period">Period</div><div class="hierarchy-chip" data-node="Duration">Duration</div>
  </div>
  <div class="hierarchy-desc" id="classDesc">Click a box above to see what it means.</div>
</div><p>Three classes <strong>hold</strong> a moment in time, one class <strong>writes and reads</strong> it as text, and two classes <strong>measure</strong> the gap between two moments. The rest of this chapter walks through them in that order.</p><div class="callout "><div class="callout-label">Importing</div><p>Each class lives in <code class="inline">java.time</code> (the formatter is in <code class="inline">java.time.format</code>). Import the ones you use, like <code class="inline">import java.time.LocalDate;</code>. If you use many, <code class="inline">import java.time.*;</code> brings in the whole package.</p></div><aside class="analogy-card"><div class="ac-head"><span class="ac-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 18h6M10 21h4M12 3a6 6 0 0 0-4 10.5c.8.8 1 1.5 1 2.5h6c0-1 .2-1.7 1-2.5A6 6 0 0 0 12 3z"/></svg></span><div><span class="ac-kicker">Think of it like…</span><span class="ac-title">A stationery box with six tools</span></div></div><p class="ac-story">Think about the tools you use to keep track of school life: the calendar on the wall, the clock above the board, your exam timetable, the way you write the date in your notebook, an age counter, and the PE teacher&rsquo;s stopwatch. Each tool does <em>one</em> job. <code>java.time</code> gives you one class per job.</p><div class="ac-map"><div class="ac-map-label">Real life → Java</div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Wall calendar</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>LocalDate</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Clock above the board</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>LocalTime</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Exam timetable entry (date and time)</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>LocalDateTime</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">The style you write the date in</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>DateTimeFormatter</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">&ldquo;You are 16 years, 4 months, 12 days old&rdquo;</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>Period</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">PE stopwatch</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>Duration</code></span></div></div></aside></section><section class="sec" id="d02"><span class="sec-num">02</span><h2><code class="inline">LocalDate</code>: representing a date</h2><p class="lede">A <code class="inline">LocalDate</code> object holds a valid date: a <strong>year</strong>, a <strong>month</strong> and a <strong>day</strong>. The <code class="inline">of()</code> method creates one from the numbers you give it.</p><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate dateObj = LocalDate.of(2019, 4, 13);
        System.out.println(dateObj);
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>2019-04-13</code></pre></div><p>The order is always <strong>year, month, day</strong>, and months are numbered <strong>1 to 12</strong> (April is <code class="inline">4</code>, not 3). Java prints a date as <code class="inline">yyyy-MM-dd</code>.</p><h3>Invalid dates are rejected</h3><p>If the date does not exist, <code class="inline">of()</code> refuses to create it and throws a <code class="inline">DateTimeException</code>.</p><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate dateObj = LocalDate.of(2022, 2, 31);
        System.out.println(dateObj);
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>Exception in thread "main" java.time.DateTimeException: Invalid date 'FEBRUARY 31'</code></pre></div><p>February never has 31 days, so Java stops right there. This is exactly the kind of runtime problem from the previous chapter: <code class="inline">DateTimeException</code> is an <strong>unchecked</strong> exception (it extends <code class="inline">RuntimeException</code>), so the compiler doesn&rsquo;t force you to catch it, but you can with <code class="inline">try/catch</code>.</p><div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="ref"><thead><tr><th>You write</th><th>Java says</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code class="inline">LocalDate.of(2023, 4, 31)</code></td><td><code>Invalid date 'APRIL 31'</code></td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">LocalDate.of(2023, 2, 29)</code></td><td><code>Invalid date 'February 29' as '2023' is not a leap year</code></td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">LocalDate.of(2023, 13, 1)</code></td><td><code>Invalid value for MonthOfYear (valid values 1 - 12): 13</code></td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">LocalDate.of(2023, 5, 32)</code></td><td><code>Invalid value for DayOfMonth (valid values 1 - 28/31): 32</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div><aside class="analogy-card"><div class="ac-head"><span class="ac-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 18h6M10 21h4M12 3a6 6 0 0 0-4 10.5c.8.8 1 1.5 1 2.5h6c0-1 .2-1.7 1-2.5A6 6 0 0 0 12 3z"/></svg></span><div><span class="ac-kicker">Think of it like…</span><span class="ac-title">One page of the school calendar</span></div></div><p class="ac-story">A calendar page shows a day, a month and a year, and nothing else: no hours, no minutes. And you will never find a page for 31 February. That day doesn&rsquo;t exist, so no printer would ever print it. <code>LocalDate.of()</code> works the same way: it only accepts days that really exist.</p><div class="ac-map"><div class="ac-map-label">Real life → Java</div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">One calendar page</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">a <code>LocalDate</code> object</span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Asking the printer for a 31 February page</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>LocalDate.of(2022, 2, 31)</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">&ldquo;That day doesn&rsquo;t exist!&rdquo;</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>DateTimeException</code></span></div></div></aside></section><section class="sec" id="d03"><span class="sec-num">03</span><h2>Today&rsquo;s date with <code class="inline">now()</code></h2><p class="lede">Don&rsquo;t want to type a date? <code class="inline">LocalDate.now()</code> reads your computer&rsquo;s clock and gives you today&rsquo;s date.</p><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate dateObj = LocalDate.now();
        System.out.println(dateObj);
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output (yours will show today's date)</span></div><pre><code>2022-12-20</code></pre></div><aside class="analogy-card"><div class="ac-head"><span class="ac-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 18h6M10 21h4M12 3a6 6 0 0 0-4 10.5c.8.8 1 1.5 1 2.5h6c0-1 .2-1.7 1-2.5A6 6 0 0 0 12 3z"/></svg></span><div><span class="ac-kicker">Think of it like…</span><span class="ac-title">Glancing at the date on your phone</span></div></div><p class="ac-story">Your phone&rsquo;s lock screen always shows <em>today&rsquo;s</em> date, so what you see changes every day. <code>LocalDate.now()</code> is the same glance, taken from inside your program. Run it tomorrow and it prints a different answer.</p><div class="ac-map"><div class="ac-map-label">Real life → Java</div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Lock-screen date</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>LocalDate.now()</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">It shows today, not a fixed day</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">the value comes from the computer&rsquo;s clock</span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Different tomorrow</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">the output isn&rsquo;t the same on every run</span></div></div></aside></section><section class="sec" id="d04"><span class="sec-num">04</span><h2>Useful methods of a <code class="inline">LocalDate</code></h2><p class="lede">Once you have a <code class="inline">LocalDate</code>, you can ask it questions and do date arithmetic.</p><div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="ref"><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What it returns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code class="inline">getYear()</code></td><td>The year (2000, 2022…)</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">getMonth()</code></td><td>The month as a name (AUGUST, SEPTEMBER…)</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">getMonthValue()</code></td><td>The month as a number (1 to 12)</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">getDayOfMonth()</code></td><td>The day of the month (1 to 31)</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">getDayOfWeek()</code></td><td>The day of the week as a name (MONDAY, TUESDAY…)</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">getDayOfWeek().getValue()</code></td><td>The day of the week as a number (MONDAY = 1 … SUNDAY = 7). <code>getValue()</code> is called on the day returned by <code>getDayOfWeek()</code>.</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">plusDays(days)</code></td><td>A new date with days added</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">plusMonths(months)</code></td><td>A new date with months added</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">plusYears(years)</code></td><td>A new date with years added</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">compareTo(date)</code></td><td>Negative if this date is earlier than the given one, positive if later, 0 if the same</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="lab" id="dateLab"><div class="lab-head"><span class="lab-tag">Live lab</span> Date Lab: build a LocalDate and poke it</div>
<div class="lab-body">
  <div class="fields">
    <label class="field">year<input type="number" id="dlY" value="2019" min="1" max="9999"></label>
    <label class="field">month<select id="dlM"></select></label>
    <label class="field">day<input type="number" id="dlD" value="13" min="1" max="40"></label>
    <label class="field">n (for plus…)<input type="number" id="dlN" value="5"></label>
  </div>
  <div class="lab-out" id="dlOut"></div>
  <p class="lab-note">Try year 2022, month 2, day 31. Then try 2024-01-31 with n = 1 for <code>plusMonths</code>.</p>
</div></div><h3>Example 1: reading the parts of a date</h3><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate dateObj = LocalDate.of(2019, 4, 13);
        System.out.println(dateObj.getYear());
        System.out.println(dateObj.getMonth());
        System.out.println(dateObj.getDayOfWeek());
        System.out.println(dateObj.getDayOfWeek().getValue());
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>2019
APRIL
SATURDAY
6</code></pre></div><h3>Example 2: adding to a date</h3><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate dateObj = LocalDate.of(2019, 4, 13);
        System.out.println(dateObj.plusYears(5));
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>2024-04-13</code></pre></div><p>Java handles month lengths and leap years for you. If adding months lands on a day that doesn&rsquo;t exist, it simply uses the last day of that month:</p><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println(LocalDate.of(2023, 1, 31).plusMonths(1));
        System.out.println(LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 31).plusMonths(1));
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>2023-02-28
2024-02-29</code></pre></div><h3>Example 3: comparing two dates</h3><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate dateObj1 = LocalDate.of(2019, 4, 13);
        LocalDate dateObj2 = LocalDate.of(2020, 4, 13);
        if (dateObj1.compareTo(dateObj2) &lt; 0) {
            System.out.println("dateObj1 &lt; dateObj2");
        } else if (dateObj1.compareTo(dateObj2) &gt; 0) {
            System.out.println("dateObj1 &gt; dateObj2");
        } else {
            System.out.println("dateObj1 == dateObj2");
        }
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>dateObj1 &lt; dateObj2</code></pre></div><p><code class="inline">dateObj1</code> is earlier than <code class="inline">dateObj2</code>, so <code class="inline">dateObj1.compareTo(dateObj2) < 0</code> is true. Only the <em>sign</em> of the result matters (negative, zero or positive), not its exact size.</p><div class="callout warn"><div class="callout-label">The one that surprises everyone: dates never change</div><p><code class="inline">plusDays()</code>, <code class="inline">plusMonths()</code> and <code class="inline">plusYears()</code> <strong>do not modify</strong> the original date. They hand back a <em>new</em> <code class="inline">LocalDate</code>. If you ignore the returned value, nothing happens:</p></div><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate x = LocalDate.of(2024, 3, 1);
        x.plusDays(10);           // result thrown away
        System.out.println(x);

        x = x.plusDays(10);       // keep the new date
        System.out.println(x);
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>2024-03-01
2024-03-11</code></pre></div><aside class="analogy-card"><div class="ac-head"><span class="ac-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 18h6M10 21h4M12 3a6 6 0 0 0-4 10.5c.8.8 1 1.5 1 2.5h6c0-1 .2-1.7 1-2.5A6 6 0 0 0 12 3z"/></svg></span><div><span class="ac-kicker">Think of it like…</span><span class="ac-title">Marking a deadline on a photocopy</span></div></div><p class="ac-story">You photocopy the calendar page and circle the date 10 days later <em>on the copy</em>. The original page on the wall hasn&rsquo;t changed at all. <code>plusDays()</code> works like that: it hands you a new date and leaves the original alone. To keep the new one, you have to put the copy somewhere (store it in a variable).</p><div class="ac-map"><div class="ac-map-label">Real life → Java</div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">The original calendar page</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>x</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">A photocopy with a date circled 10 days later</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>x.plusDays(10)</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Putting the copy in your folder</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>x = x.plusDays(10);</code></span></div></div></aside></section><section class="sec" id="d05"><span class="sec-num">05</span><h2><code class="inline">LocalTime</code>: representing a time</h2><p class="lede">A <code class="inline">LocalTime</code> holds a valid time of day: <strong>hours</strong>, <strong>minutes</strong> and <strong>seconds</strong>, with no date attached.</p><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalTime;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalTime timeObj = LocalTime.of(11, 34, 56);
        System.out.println(timeObj);
        System.out.println(timeObj.getHour());
        System.out.println(timeObj.getMinute());
        System.out.println(timeObj.getSecond());
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>11:34:56
11
34
56</code></pre></div><p>Hours use the <strong>24-hour clock</strong> (0 to 23), so 1 PM is <code class="inline">13</code>. Out-of-range values such as <code class="inline">LocalTime.of(25, 0)</code> throw a <code class="inline">DateTimeException</code> too.</p><div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="ref"><thead><tr><th>Method</th><th>What it returns</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code class="inline">now()</code></td><td>The current time</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">getHour()</code></td><td>The hour value</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">getMinute()</code></td><td>The minute value</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">getSecond()</code></td><td>The second value</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">plusHours(hours)</code></td><td>A new time with hours added</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">plusMinutes(minutes)</code></td><td>A new time with minutes added</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">plusSeconds(seconds)</code></td><td>A new time with seconds added</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">compareTo(time)</code></td><td>Negative if this time is earlier than the given one, positive if later, 0 if the same</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="lab" id="timeLab"><div class="lab-head"><span class="lab-tag">Live lab</span> Time Machine: add time to a LocalTime</div>
<div class="lab-body">
  <div class="fields">
    <label class="field">time<input type="time" id="tlT" value="11:34:56" step="1"></label>
    <label class="field">n (for plus…)<input type="number" id="tlN" value="15"></label>
  </div>
  <div class="lab-out" id="tlOut"></div>
  <p class="lab-note">Start at 23:00 and add 2 hours. Notice there is no &ldquo;next day&rdquo;: a <code>LocalTime</code> just wraps around.</p>
</div></div><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalTime;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalTime timeObj = LocalTime.of(11, 34, 56);
        System.out.println(timeObj.plusMinutes(45));
        System.out.println(timeObj.plusHours(15));
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>12:19:56
02:34:56</code></pre></div><p>Adding 15 hours to 11:34 passes midnight, so the answer wraps around to <code class="inline">02:34:56</code>. A time has no date, so there is no &ldquo;next day&rdquo; to remember.</p><div class="callout "><div class="callout-label">Zero seconds are not printed</div><p><code class="inline">LocalTime.of(9, 5, 0)</code> prints as <code class="inline">09:05</code>, not <code class="inline">09:05:00</code>. It&rsquo;s the same time; Java just leaves off seconds that are zero.</p></div><aside class="analogy-card"><div class="ac-head"><span class="ac-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 18h6M10 21h4M12 3a6 6 0 0 0-4 10.5c.8.8 1 1.5 1 2.5h6c0-1 .2-1.7 1-2.5A6 6 0 0 0 12 3z"/></svg></span><div><span class="ac-kicker">Think of it like…</span><span class="ac-title">The clock above the blackboard</span></div></div><p class="ac-story">A wall clock shows hours, minutes and seconds but <em>no day</em>. After 11:59 PM its hands simply carry on from 12:00 AM. <code>LocalTime</code> behaves the same: add 15 hours to 11:34 and it wraps around past midnight to 02:34.</p><div class="ac-map"><div class="ac-map-label">Real life → Java</div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">The wall clock</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>LocalTime</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Hands passing 12 and starting again</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>plusHours()</code> wrapping past midnight</span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">A clock has no calendar</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">a <code>LocalTime</code> has no date part</span></div></div></aside></section><section class="sec" id="d06"><span class="sec-num">06</span><h2><code class="inline">LocalDateTime</code>: date and time together</h2><p class="lede">When you need both a date <em>and</em> a time (an exam start, a bus departure, a log entry), use <code class="inline">LocalDateTime</code>.</p><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDateTime;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDateTime dateTimeObj = LocalDateTime.of(2018, 11, 28, 10, 15, 26);
        System.out.println(dateTimeObj);
        System.out.println(dateTimeObj.getYear());
        System.out.println(dateTimeObj.getMonthValue());
        System.out.println(dateTimeObj.getHour());
        System.out.println(dateTimeObj.getMinute());
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>2018-11-28T10:15:26
2018
11
10
15</code></pre></div><p>The arguments are <code class="inline">year, month, day, hour, minute, second</code>. When printed, the date and time are joined by the letter <code class="inline">T</code>. A <code class="inline">LocalDateTime</code> has all the getters of a <code class="inline">LocalDate</code> (<code class="inline">getYear()</code>, <code class="inline">getMonth()</code>&hellip;) <em>and</em> of a <code class="inline">LocalTime</code> (<code class="inline">getHour()</code>, <code class="inline">getMinute()</code>&hellip;), and <code class="inline">LocalDateTime.now()</code> gives you the current date and time.</p><aside class="analogy-card"><div class="ac-head"><span class="ac-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 18h6M10 21h4M12 3a6 6 0 0 0-4 10.5c.8.8 1 1.5 1 2.5h6c0-1 .2-1.7 1-2.5A6 6 0 0 0 12 3z"/></svg></span><div><span class="ac-kicker">Think of it like…</span><span class="ac-title">An exam admit card</span></div></div><p class="ac-story">Your admit card doesn&rsquo;t just say &ldquo;Maths, 9:30 AM&rdquo; (that could be any day) or just &ldquo;14 March&rdquo; (that could be any time). It lists both: <strong>14 March, 9:30 AM</strong>. <code>LocalDateTime</code> is that complete entry: a date and a time together.</p><div class="ac-map"><div class="ac-map-label">Real life → Java</div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">&ldquo;14 March&rdquo;</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">the date part (like <code>LocalDate</code>)</span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">&ldquo;9:30 AM&rdquo;</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">the time part (like <code>LocalTime</code>)</span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">The whole line on the admit card</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>LocalDateTime</code></span></div></div></aside></section><section class="sec" id="d07"><span class="sec-num">07</span><h2><code class="inline">DateTimeFormatter</code>: formatting</h2><p class="lede">The default look of a date (<code class="inline">2022-09-13</code>) isn&rsquo;t always what you want to show people. <code class="inline">DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern()</code> builds a formatter from a <strong>pattern</strong> made of letters like <code class="inline">dd</code>, <code class="inline">MMM</code> and <code class="inline">yyyy</code>, and <code class="inline">format()</code> uses it to turn a date or time into a <code class="inline">String</code>.</p><div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="ref"><thead><tr><th>Pattern</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Example</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code class="inline">yyyy</code></td><td>year with century</td><td>2014, 2015</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">yy</code></td><td>year without century</td><td>14, 15</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">MMMM</code></td><td>month as full name</td><td>January, February</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">MMM</code></td><td>month as abbreviated name</td><td>Jan, Feb</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">MM</code></td><td>month, zero-padded</td><td>01, 02, …, 12</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">M</code></td><td>month, no padding</td><td>1, 2, …, 12</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">dd</code></td><td>day of month, zero-padded</td><td>01, 02, …, 31</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">d</code></td><td>day of month, no padding</td><td>1, 2, …, 31</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">a</code></td><td>AM or PM</td><td>AM, PM</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">HH</code></td><td>hour on the 24-hour clock, zero-padded</td><td>00, 01, …, 23</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">H</code></td><td>hour on the 24-hour clock, no padding</td><td>0, 1, …, 23</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">hh</code></td><td>hour on the 12-hour clock, zero-padded</td><td>01, 02, …, 12</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">h</code></td><td>hour on the 12-hour clock, no padding</td><td>1, 2, …, 12</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">mm</code></td><td>minute, zero-padded</td><td>00, 01, …, 59</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">m</code></td><td>minute, no padding</td><td>0, 1, …, 59</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">ss</code></td><td>second, zero-padded</td><td>00, 01, …, 59</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">s</code></td><td>second, no padding</td><td>0, 1, …, 59</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout warn"><div class="callout-label">Capital or small? It matters!</div><p><code class="inline">MM</code> is the <strong>month</strong>, but <code class="inline">mm</code> is the <strong>minute</strong>. <code class="inline">HH</code> is the 24-hour clock; <code class="inline">hh</code> is the 12-hour clock (use it together with <code class="inline">a</code> so people know AM from PM). Also write <code class="inline">yyyy</code>, not <code class="inline">YYYY</code>: the capital version means something different (a &ldquo;week-based year&rdquo;) and gives the wrong year for some dates near New Year, such as 30 December 2024 printing as 2025.</p></div><div class="lab" id="fmtLab"><div class="lab-head"><span class="lab-tag">Live lab</span> Pattern Lab: write a pattern, see the result</div>
<div class="lab-body">
  <div class="fields">
    <label class="field">date and time<input type="datetime-local" id="flDT" value="2022-09-13T13:23:46" step="1"></label>
    <label class="field">pattern<input type="text" id="flP" class="wide" value="dd MMM yyyy hh:mm:ss a" spellcheck="false" autocomplete="off"></label>
  </div>
  <div class="presets" id="flPresets"></div>
  <div class="big-preview" id="flOut"></div>
  <div class="tokens" id="flTokens"></div>
  <p class="lab-note">Each coloured label is one piece of the pattern, with what it turned into underneath. Try <code>mm/dd/yyyy</code> (lowercase!) and see what happens.</p>
</div></div><h3>Example 1: date and time</h3><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDateTime now = LocalDateTime.of(2022, 9, 13, 13, 23, 46);
        DateTimeFormatter format1 = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd MMM yyyy hh:mm:ss a");
        String formattedDateTime = now.format(format1);
        System.out.println(formattedDateTime);
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>13 Sep 2022 01:23:46 PM</code></pre></div><p>We used a fixed date-time here so the output is always the same. Swap in <code class="inline">LocalDateTime.now()</code> to format the current moment instead.</p><h3>Example 2: just a date</h3><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate now = LocalDate.of(2022, 9, 13);
        DateTimeFormatter format1 = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd MMMM yyyy");
        String formattedDate = now.format(format1);
        System.out.println(formattedDate);
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>13 September 2022</code></pre></div><p><code class="inline">format()</code> works on <code class="inline">LocalDate</code>, <code class="inline">LocalTime</code> and <code class="inline">LocalDateTime</code>, but the pattern must only use letters the object has. A <code class="inline">LocalDate</code> has no hours, so formatting it with <code class="inline">HH:mm</code> throws an <code class="inline">UnsupportedTemporalTypeException</code>.</p><h3>The classic slip: <code class="inline">mm</code> vs <code class="inline">MM</code></h3><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDateTime;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDateTime t = LocalDateTime.of(2022, 9, 13, 13, 23, 46);
        System.out.println(t.format(DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("mm/dd/yyyy")));
        System.out.println(t.format(DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("MM/dd/yyyy")));
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>23/13/2022
09/13/2022</code></pre></div><p>The first line shows <strong>23</strong> where the month should be: that&rsquo;s the <em>minutes</em> (13:<strong>23</strong>:46). Java doesn&rsquo;t complain; it just prints the wrong thing.</p><aside class="analogy-card"><div class="ac-head"><span class="ac-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 18h6M10 21h4M12 3a6 6 0 0 0-4 10.5c.8.8 1 1.5 1 2.5h6c0-1 .2-1.7 1-2.5A6 6 0 0 0 12 3z"/></svg></span><div><span class="ac-kicker">Think of it like…</span><span class="ac-title">One birthday, many ways to write it</span></div></div><p class="ac-story">The same day can be written <strong>13/09/2022</strong>, <strong>09/13/2022</strong>, <strong>13 Sep 2022</strong> or <strong>September 13, 2022</strong>. The day itself never changed, only how it&rsquo;s written down. A <code>DateTimeFormatter</code> is your &ldquo;writing style&rdquo;: you pick the style once (the pattern) and Java writes dates that way.</p><div class="ac-map"><div class="ac-map-label">Real life → Java</div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">The actual day</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">a <code>LocalDate</code> object</span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Your chosen writing style</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern(&hellip;)</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Writing the day down in that style</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>date.format(formatter)</code></span></div></div></aside></section><section class="sec" id="d08"><span class="sec-num">08</span><h2>Parsing: turning text into a date</h2><p class="lede">Formatting goes from object to text. <strong>Parsing</strong> goes the other way: from a <code class="inline">String</code> to a date, time or date-time object. You give <code class="inline">parse()</code> the text and a formatter that describes how the text is written.</p><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String dateStr = "28 November 2018";
        DateTimeFormatter format1 = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd MMMM yyyy");
        LocalDate date = LocalDate.parse(dateStr, format1);
        System.out.println(date);
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>2018-11-28</code></pre></div><p>The pattern must describe the text <em>exactly</em>. The same text can mean two different dates depending on the pattern:</p><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        String text = "03/04/2024";
        System.out.println(LocalDate.parse(text, DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("dd/MM/yyyy")));
        System.out.println(LocalDate.parse(text, DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("MM/dd/yyyy")));
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>2024-04-03
2024-03-04</code></pre></div><p>And when the text doesn&rsquo;t fit the pattern, Java refuses with a <code class="inline">DateTimeParseException</code> instead of guessing:</p><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.format.DateTimeFormatter;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        DateTimeFormatter f = DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("MM/dd/yyyy");
        LocalDate d = LocalDate.parse("28/11/2018", f);
        System.out.println(d);
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>Exception in thread "main" java.time.format.DateTimeParseException: Text '28/11/2018' could not be parsed: Invalid value for MonthOfYear (valid values 1 - 12): 28</code></pre></div><p>The pattern said the first number is the month, and there is no month 28. Like <code class="inline">DateTimeException</code>, this exception is unchecked, so <code class="inline">try/catch</code> is how you protect your program when the text comes from a user.</p><div class="callout "><div class="callout-label">Shortcut for the standard format</div><p>If the text is already in Java&rsquo;s own <code class="inline">yyyy-MM-dd</code> style, you can skip the formatter: <code class="inline">LocalDate.parse("2018-11-28")</code> just works. Same for <code class="inline">LocalTime.parse("09:30")</code>.</p></div><aside class="analogy-card"><div class="ac-head"><span class="ac-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 18h6M10 21h4M12 3a6 6 0 0 0-4 10.5c.8.8 1 1.5 1 2.5h6c0-1 .2-1.7 1-2.5A6 6 0 0 0 12 3z"/></svg></span><div><span class="ac-kicker">Think of it like…</span><span class="ac-title">Reading a friend&rsquo;s date: 03/04, March or April?</span></div></div><p class="ac-story">Your friend texts <strong>03/04/2024</strong>. Is that 3 April or 4 March? You can&rsquo;t tell without knowing <em>their</em> writing style. Java has exactly the same problem, so you must tell it the style. If the text doesn&rsquo;t fit the style, Java refuses rather than guessing.</p><div class="ac-map"><div class="ac-map-label">Real life → Java</div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Your friend&rsquo;s text message</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">a <code>String</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Knowing their writing style</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">the pattern in a <code>DateTimeFormatter</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Working out the real date</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>LocalDate.parse(text, formatter)</code></span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Text that can&rsquo;t be that style</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>DateTimeParseException</code></span></div></div></aside></section><section class="sec" id="d09"><span class="sec-num">09</span><h2><code class="inline">Period</code>: difference between dates</h2><p class="lede">How long from one date to another? <code class="inline">Period.between(start, end)</code> answers in calendar units: <strong>years, months and days</strong>.</p><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;
import java.time.Period;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate startDate = LocalDate.of(2020, 2, 20);
        LocalDate endDate = LocalDate.of(2021, 10, 21);
        Period period = Period.between(startDate, endDate);
        System.out.println("Years: " + period.getYears());
        System.out.println("Months: " + period.getMonths());
        System.out.println("Days: " + period.getDays());
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>Years: 1
Months: 8
Days: 1</code></pre></div><p>From 20 Feb 2020 to 21 Oct 2021 is 1 year (to 20 Feb 2021), plus 8 months (to 20 Oct 2021), plus 1 more day. Notice the result is split into three separate numbers; <code class="inline">getDays()</code> is <em>not</em> the total number of days.</p><div class="lab" id="periodLab"><div class="lab-head"><span class="lab-tag">Live lab</span> Period Lab: gap between two dates</div>
<div class="lab-body">
  <div class="fields">
    <label class="field">start date<input type="date" id="plA" value="2020-02-20"></label>
    <label class="field">end date<input type="date" id="plB" value="2021-10-21"></label>
  </div>
  <div class="lab-out" id="plOut"></div>
  <p class="lab-note">Swap the dates and the numbers turn negative. <code>Period.between(start, end)</code> counts from the first to the second.</p>
</div></div><aside class="analogy-card"><div class="ac-head"><span class="ac-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 18h6M10 21h4M12 3a6 6 0 0 0-4 10.5c.8.8 1 1.5 1 2.5h6c0-1 .2-1.7 1-2.5A6 6 0 0 0 12 3z"/></svg></span><div><span class="ac-kicker">Think of it like…</span><span class="ac-title">Your age in years, months and days</span></div></div><p class="ac-story">When someone asks how old you are, you can answer &ldquo;16 years, 4 months and 12 days.&rdquo; That&rsquo;s counted in <em>calendar</em> units. <code>Period.between()</code> gives exactly that kind of answer for any two dates.</p><div class="ac-map"><div class="ac-map-label">Real life → Java</div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Your birthday</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">the start date</span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Today</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">the end date</span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">&ldquo;16 years, 4 months, 12 days&rdquo;</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">a <code>Period</code> (<code>getYears()</code>, <code>getMonths()</code>, <code>getDays()</code>)</span></div></div></aside></section><section class="sec" id="d10"><span class="sec-num">10</span><h2><code class="inline">Duration</code>: difference between times</h2><p class="lede">For the gap between two <em>times</em>, use <code class="inline">Duration.between(start, end)</code>. It measures exact length of time, and <code class="inline">getSeconds()</code> tells you how many seconds that is.</p><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.Duration;
import java.time.LocalTime;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalTime startTime = LocalTime.of(10, 30, 30);
        LocalTime endTime = LocalTime.of(10, 31, 30);
        Duration duration = Duration.between(startTime, endTime);
        System.out.println(duration.getSeconds());
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>60</code></pre></div><p>Seconds are not always the most convenient unit, so <code class="inline">Duration</code> can also report whole minutes and hours:</p><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Main.java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.Duration;
import java.time.LocalTime;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        Duration school = Duration.between(LocalTime.of(8, 0), LocalTime.of(17, 30));
        System.out.println(school.getSeconds());
        System.out.println(school.toMinutes());
        System.out.println(school.toHours());
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>34200
570
9</code></pre></div><div class="lab" id="durLab"><div class="lab-head"><span class="lab-tag">Live lab</span> Stopwatch Lab: gap between two times</div>
<div class="lab-body">
  <div class="fields">
    <label class="field">start time<input type="time" id="dulA" value="10:30:30" step="1"></label>
    <label class="field">end time<input type="time" id="dulB" value="10:31:30" step="1"></label>
  </div>
  <div class="lab-out" id="dulOut"></div>
</div></div><div class="callout warn"><div class="callout-label">Match the tool to the type</div><p><code class="inline">Period</code> is for <strong>dates</strong> and <code class="inline">Duration</code> is for <strong>times</strong> (or date-times). Asking <code class="inline">Duration.between()</code> for the gap between two plain <code class="inline">LocalDate</code> objects fails with an <code class="inline">UnsupportedTemporalTypeException</code>, because a date has no seconds to count.</p></div><aside class="analogy-card"><div class="ac-head"><span class="ac-icon"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="M9 18h6M10 21h4M12 3a6 6 0 0 0-4 10.5c.8.8 1 1.5 1 2.5h6c0-1 .2-1.7 1-2.5A6 6 0 0 0 12 3z"/></svg></span><div><span class="ac-kicker">Think of it like…</span><span class="ac-title">The stopwatch on sports day</span></div></div><p class="ac-story">A stopwatch doesn&rsquo;t care about months or calendars. It counts exact seconds from the start button to the stop button. <code>Duration</code> works that way: it measures the exact length of time between two times.</p><div class="ac-map"><div class="ac-map-label">Real life → Java</div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Pressing START</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">the start time</span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">Pressing STOP</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java">the end time</span></div><div class="ac-row"><span class="ac-real">The number on the display</span><span class="ac-arrow">→</span><span class="ac-java"><code>duration.getSeconds()</code></span></div></div></aside></section><section class="sec" id="d11"><span class="sec-num">11</span><h2>Choosing the right class</h2><p class="lede">With six classes it&rsquo;s easy to grab the wrong one. Start from what you want to <em>do</em>, and the right class follows.</p><div class="lab" id="pickLab"><div class="lab-head"><span class="lab-tag">Try it</span> Which class do I need?</div>
<div class="lab-body">
  <div class="pick-goals" id="pickGoals"></div>
  <div class="pick-result" id="pickResult"><p>Pick a goal above.</p></div>
</div></div><div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="ref"><thead><tr><th>I want to…</th><th>Use</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Store a birthday, a deadline, a holiday</td><td><code class="inline">LocalDate</code></td></tr><tr><td>Store a bell time, an alarm time</td><td><code class="inline">LocalTime</code></td></tr><tr><td>Store an exact moment (date <em>and</em> time)</td><td><code class="inline">LocalDateTime</code></td></tr><tr><td>Show a date or time in a particular style</td><td><code class="inline">DateTimeFormatter</code> + <code class="inline">format()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Turn text typed by a user into a date or time</td><td><code class="inline">DateTimeFormatter</code> + <code class="inline">parse()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Find years / months / days between two dates</td><td><code class="inline">Period.between()</code></td></tr><tr><td>Find seconds between two times</td><td><code class="inline">Duration.between()</code></td></tr></tbody></table></div></section><section class="sec" id="d12"><span class="sec-num">12</span><h2>Common mistakes</h2><ul class="checklist"><li><span class="x">✕</span><span><strong>Counting months from 0.</strong> January is <code class="inline">1</code> and December is <code class="inline">12</code>. <code class="inline">LocalDate.of(2023, 0, 5)</code> is an error.</span></li><li><span class="x">✕</span><span><strong>Forgetting that dates never change.</strong> <code class="inline">date.plusDays(5);</code> on its own does nothing. Store the result: <code class="inline">date = date.plusDays(5);</code>.</span></li><li><span class="x">✕</span><span><strong>Mixing up <code class="inline">MM</code> and <code class="inline">mm</code>.</strong> Capital <code class="inline">MM</code> is the month, small <code class="inline">mm</code> is the minute. The wrong one prints silently, with no error.</span></li><li><span class="x">✕</span><span><strong>Parsing with the wrong pattern.</strong> The pattern has to match the text exactly: <code class="inline">dd/MM/yyyy</code> for &ldquo;28/11/2018&rdquo;, not <code class="inline">MM/dd/yyyy</code>.</span></li><li><span class="x">✕</span><span><strong>Using time letters on a <code class="inline">LocalDate</code>.</strong> A date has no hours, so <code class="inline">HH:mm</code> throws an exception. Use a <code class="inline">LocalDateTime</code> if you need both.</span></li><li><span class="x">✕</span><span><strong>Using <code class="inline">Duration</code> for dates (or <code class="inline">Period</code> for times).</strong> <code class="inline">Period</code> is for dates, <code class="inline">Duration</code> is for times.</span></li><li><span class="x">✕</span><span><strong>Forgetting the import.</strong> <code class="inline">LocalDate</code>, <code class="inline">Period</code> and the rest all need an <code class="inline">import</code> (or <code class="inline">import java.time.*;</code>).</span></li></ul></section><section class="sec" id="d13"><span class="sec-num">13</span><h2>Try it: nine quick challenges</h2><p class="lede">Work each one out yourself before revealing the answer.</p><div class="challenge"><div class="challenge-head"><span class="challenge-tag">Spot the problem</span> Will this run?</div><div class="challenge-body"><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>LocalDate d = LocalDate.of(2023, 4, 31);</code></pre></div><p>What happens when this line runs?</p><button class="reveal-btn">Reveal answer <span class="chev">▾</span></button><div class="answer-panel"><p>It throws <code class="inline">DateTimeException: Invalid date 'APRIL 31'</code>. April has only 30 days.</p></div></div></div><div class="challenge"><div class="challenge-head"><span class="challenge-tag">Guess the output</span> Month-end maths</div><div class="challenge-body"><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>LocalDate d = LocalDate.of(2024, 1, 31);
System.out.println(d.plusMonths(1));</code></pre></div><p>Does it print an error, or a date? Which one?</p><button class="reveal-btn">Reveal answer <span class="chev">▾</span></button><div class="answer-panel"><p><code class="inline">2024-02-29</code>. There is no 31 February, so Java uses the last day of the month, and 2024 is a leap year.</p></div></div></div><div class="challenge"><div class="challenge-head"><span class="challenge-tag">Guess the output</span> The unchanged date</div><div class="challenge-body"><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>LocalDate x = LocalDate.of(2024, 3, 1);
x.plusDays(10);
System.out.println(x);</code></pre></div><p>What prints?</p><button class="reveal-btn">Reveal answer <span class="chev">▾</span></button><div class="answer-panel"><p><code class="inline">2024-03-01</code>. The new date returned by <code class="inline">plusDays(10)</code> was thrown away, and <code class="inline">x</code> was never changed. You&rsquo;d need <code class="inline">x = x.plusDays(10);</code>.</p></div></div></div><div class="challenge"><div class="challenge-head"><span class="challenge-tag">Pick the pattern</span> Write the pattern</div><div class="challenge-body"><p>Today is 13 September 2022. Write the pattern that produces each text:</p><p>(a) <code class="inline">13 Sep 2022</code> &nbsp; (b) <code class="inline">09/13/2022</code> &nbsp; (c) <code class="inline">1:23 PM</code></p><button class="reveal-btn">Reveal answer <span class="chev">▾</span></button><div class="answer-panel"><p>(a) <code class="inline">dd MMM yyyy</code> &nbsp; (b) <code class="inline">MM/dd/yyyy</code> &nbsp; (c) <code class="inline">h:mm a</code> (at 13:23 on the 12-hour clock).</p></div></div></div><div class="challenge"><div class="challenge-head"><span class="challenge-tag">Spot the mistake</span> Why is the month 23?</div><div class="challenge-body"><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>LocalDateTime t = LocalDateTime.of(2022, 9, 13, 13, 23, 46);
System.out.println(t.format(DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("mm/dd/yyyy")));</code></pre></div><p>Someone wanted <code class="inline">09/13/2022</code> but got <code class="inline">23/13/2022</code>. What went wrong?</p><button class="reveal-btn">Reveal answer <span class="chev">▾</span></button><div class="answer-panel"><p>Small <code class="inline">mm</code> means <strong>minutes</strong> (the time was 13:<strong>23</strong>:46). The month needs capital <code class="inline">MM</code>.</p></div></div></div><div class="challenge"><div class="challenge-head"><span class="challenge-tag">Parse it</span> Will this parse?</div><div class="challenge-body"><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>LocalDate d = LocalDate.parse("28/11/2018",
        DateTimeFormatter.ofPattern("MM/dd/yyyy"));</code></pre></div><p>Does this work? If not, how do you fix it?</p><button class="reveal-btn">Reveal answer <span class="chev">▾</span></button><div class="answer-panel"><p>No: <code class="inline">DateTimeParseException</code>. The pattern says the first number is the month, and there is no month 28. Use <code class="inline">dd/MM/yyyy</code>.</p></div></div></div><div class="challenge"><div class="challenge-head"><span class="challenge-tag">Predict the result</span> How far apart?</div><div class="challenge-body"><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>LocalDate a = LocalDate.of(2023, 1, 15);
LocalDate b = LocalDate.of(2023, 3, 10);
Period p = Period.between(a, b);</code></pre></div><p>What are <code class="inline">p.getYears()</code>, <code class="inline">p.getMonths()</code> and <code class="inline">p.getDays()</code>?</p><button class="reveal-btn">Reveal answer <span class="chev">▾</span></button><div class="answer-panel"><p><strong>0 years, 1 month, 23 days.</strong> 15 January + 1 month is 15 February; from 15 February to 10 March is 23 days (13 days left in a 28-day February, plus 10).</p></div></div></div><div class="challenge"><div class="challenge-head"><span class="challenge-tag">Predict the result</span> Seconds on the stopwatch</div><div class="challenge-body"><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>LocalTime a = LocalTime.of(9, 15, 0);
LocalTime b = LocalTime.of(10, 45, 30);
System.out.println(Duration.between(a, b).getSeconds());</code></pre></div><p>What prints?</p><button class="reveal-btn">Reveal answer <span class="chev">▾</span></button><div class="answer-panel"><p><code class="inline">5430</code>. That is 1 hour (3600) + 30 minutes (1800) + 30 seconds.</p></div></div></div><div class="challenge"><div class="challenge-head"><span class="challenge-tag">Try it yourself</span> Birthday weekday</div><div class="challenge-body"><p>Write code that prints the <strong>day of the week</strong> that 15 August 2030 falls on.</p><button class="reveal-btn">Reveal one possible answer <span class="chev">▾</span></button><div class="answer-panel"><div class="codeblock"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Java</span><button class="copy-btn">Copy</button></div><pre><code>import java.time.LocalDate;

class Main {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        LocalDate d = LocalDate.of(2030, 8, 15);
        System.out.println(d.getDayOfWeek());
    }
}</code></pre></div><div class="codeblock out small"><div class="cb-head"><span class="cb-lang">Output</span></div><pre><code>THURSDAY</code></pre></div></div></div></div></section><section class="sec" id="d14"><span class="sec-num">14</span><h2>Final self-test</h2><p class="lede">Try answering these without scrolling back up.</p><div class="selftest-bar"><span id="selftestCount">0 / 6 answered</span><div class="selftest-track"><div class="selftest-fill" id="selftestFill"></div></div></div><div id="selftestList"></div></section><section class="sec" id="d15"><span class="sec-num">15</span><h2>Chapter recap</h2><div class="recap-grid"><div class="recap-card"><h4>LocalDate</h4><p>A date: year, month, day. Create with <code class="inline">of()</code> or <code class="inline">now()</code>.</p></div><div class="recap-card"><h4>LocalTime</h4><p>A time of day: hour, minute, second. Wraps around at midnight.</p></div><div class="recap-card"><h4>LocalDateTime</h4><p>A date and a time together. Printed with a <code class="inline">T</code> between them.</p></div><div class="recap-card"><h4>plus… / compareTo</h4><p><code class="inline">plusDays</code> and friends return a <em>new</em> object. <code class="inline">compareTo</code> tells earlier, later or equal.</p></div><div class="recap-card"><h4>DateTimeFormatter</h4><p><code class="inline">ofPattern()</code> builds the style. <code class="inline">format()</code> writes text, <code class="inline">parse()</code> reads it.</p></div><div class="recap-card"><h4>Pattern letters</h4><p><code class="inline">MM</code> month, <code class="inline">mm</code> minute, <code class="inline">HH</code> 24-hour, <code class="inline">hh</code> 12-hour with <code class="inline">a</code>.</p></div><div class="recap-card"><h4>Period</h4><p>Gap between two dates as years, months and days.</p></div><div class="recap-card"><h4>Duration</h4><p>Gap between two times; <code class="inline">getSeconds()</code> for the length.</p></div></div><h3>Quick reference</h3><div class="tbl-wrap"><table class="ref"><thead><tr><th>Class</th><th>Package</th><th>Use it for</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code class="inline">LocalDate</code></td><td>java.time</td><td>A date only</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">LocalTime</code></td><td>java.time</td><td>A time only</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">LocalDateTime</code></td><td>java.time</td><td>A date and a time</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">DateTimeFormatter</code></td><td>java.time.format</td><td>Formatting and parsing text</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">Period</code></td><td>java.time</td><td>Difference between dates</td></tr><tr><td><code class="inline">Duration</code></td><td>java.time</td><td>Difference between times</td></tr></tbody></table></div><div class="callout "><div class="callout-label">⭐ The easiest sentence to remember</div><p><strong>Pick <code class="inline">LocalDate</code>, <code class="inline">LocalTime</code> or <code class="inline">LocalDateTime</code> to hold the moment, <code class="inline">DateTimeFormatter</code> to write or read it as text, and <code class="inline">Period</code> (dates) or <code class="inline">Duration</code> (times) to measure the gap. Remember that dates never change: every <code class="inline">plus…</code> gives you a new one.</strong></p></div></section>
